## Best Practices

### Thread Safety
- Always use `Arc<Mutex<>>` for shared state
- Clone tools before async operations to avoid holding locks
- Use proper error handling with `map_err`

### Error Handling
```rust
// Good: Detailed error messages
.map_err(|e| format!("Failed to initialize your system: {}", e))?

// Good: Logging at appropriate levels
debug!("Starting operation with param: {}", param);
info!("Operation completed successfully");
warn!("Non-critical issue: {}", warning);
```

### Async Patterns
```rust
// Good: Don't hold locks across await
let tools = {
    let guard = TOOLS.lock().map_err(|e| format!("Lock error: {}", e))?;
    guard.clone()
};
let result = tools.async_operation().await?;

// Bad: Holding lock across await (not Send)
let tools = TOOLS.lock().map_err(|e| format!("Lock error: {}", e))?;
let result = tools.async_operation().await?; // Compilation error
```

### Tool Definitions
- Use clear, descriptive names and descriptions
- Define proper JSON schemas for parameters
- Include all required fields in the schema
- Use appropriate data types (string, number, boolean, object, array)

### Performance
- Implement caching for expensive operations
- Use lazy initialization where appropriate
- Clean up resources when no longer needed
- Minimize memory footprint

## Testing Patterns

### Unit Tests
```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn test_your_tool() {
        let tools = YourTools::new();
        let result = tools.your_method("test_param".to_string()).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(result.expected_field, "expected_value");
    }
}
```

### Integration Tests
- Test with real Tauri app handle when possible
- Verify tool registration works correctly
- Test error handling and edge cases
- Validate tool definitions are correct

